EXT. PUBLIC BOCCE COURT - TWILIGHT

A crushed-oyster-shell lane between two wooden rails. String lights droop from a mulberry tree. A cooler of beer rests on a bench.

The last frame of a long match. Four players stand at the near end.

NONNA ALBERTINA FERRANTE-OKOYE (70s, apron over Sunday dress) holds a red ball, rolling it in her palm. Her partner, SALVATORE "SAL THE HAND" DRUMMOND (60s, straw hat, cigar unlit) leans on his cane. Their rivals: WALTER BRENNAN-ISHIDA (60s, golf visor) stands rigid with a green ball, and his daughter CLEMENTINE (30s, headphones around her neck) chews a toothpick and watches the pallino sitting at the far end.

ALBERTINA
Everybody quiet.

SAL
We're quiet.

WALTER
I'm not breathing.

CLEMENTINE
(to Walter)
Dad, you're breathing.

Albertina steps to the line. Bends her knees. Swings. The red ball rolls, wobbles, bumps a rail, slows, and kisses the pallino. A hush. Then it rolls on, a quarter inch, and drifts to a stop precisely against it.

SAL
(to the group)
Did you see that? Did you all see that?

WALTER
I saw it.

CLEMENTINE
We all saw it.

Walter lays his green ball gently on the bench. He pulls off his visor, wipes his forehead with the back of his wrist, and puts it on again, backward.

ALBERTINA
Walter. Fine game.

WALTER
Albertina. Fifty years I've wanted to say that to you.

ALBERTINA
Say what?

WALTER
Fine game.

They shake hands. Sal lights the cigar at last. Clementine looks at the pallino, at the red ball against it, at her father's backward visor, and begins, helplessly, to laugh.
